ARTICLE 9: LIABILITY AND INDEMNITY

9.1 Subject to Clause 9.2, each Party shall be liable to the other Party for any direct loss or damage arising out of or in connection with this Agreement, whether in contract, tort (including negligence), breach of statutory duty, or otherwise.

9.2 Neither Party shall be liable to the other Party, whether in contract, tort (including negligence), breach of statutory duty, or otherwise, for any:
(a) loss of profits;
(b) loss of business or business opportunity;
(c) loss of anticipated savings;
(d) loss of goodwill or reputation; or
(e) indirect or consequential loss or damage,
arising out of or in connection with this Agreement.

9.3 Subject to Clause 9.4, the aggregate liability of either Party arising out of or in connection with this Agreement, whether in contract, tort (including negligence), breach of statutory duty, or otherwise, shall not exceed the total fees paid or payable by the Client to the Provider during the twelve (12) months preceding the date on which the cause of action arose.

9.4 Nothing in this Agreement shall operate to exclude or limit either Party's liability for:
(a) death or personal injury caused by negligence;
(b) fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation;
(c) any breach of the obligations set out in Article 10 (Confidentiality) or Article 11 (Data Protection);
(d) any liability which cannot lawfully be excluded or limited under applicable law; or
(e) the Provider's indemnity obligations under Clause 9.5.

9.5 The Provider shall indemnify and hold harmless the Client against all losses, claims, damages, costs, and expenses (including reasonable legal fees) awarded against or incurred by the Client arising out of or in connection with:
(a) any claim that the provision of the Services or the Provider's use of personnel infringes any third party's intellectual property rights;
(b) any claim brought by any personnel supplied by the Provider (including any claim for unpaid wages, benefits, or statutory contributions, or any claim arising from the termination of such personnel's engagement) to the extent that such claim arises from the Provider's failure to comply with its obligations under this Agreement or applicable law;
(c) any breach by the Provider of any applicable law or regulation, including the Nigerian Labour Act, Cap L1, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 2004, and any social security or pension legislation; and
(d) any negligent act or omission, or wilful misconduct, of the Provider, its personnel, or any subcontractor engaged by the Provider in connection with the Services.

9.6 The Client shall promptly notify the Provider in writing of any claim or potential claim for which it seeks an indemnity under Clause 9.5, and shall:
(a) provide the Provider with reasonable information and assistance in relation to such claim; and
(b) not make any admission of liability or settle or compromise any such claim without the Provider's prior written consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld or delayed.

9.7 The Provider shall maintain, at its own cost, appropriate insurance policies with reputable insurers, including:
(a) employer's liability insurance with a limit of not less than Naira Fifty Million (₦50,000,000) per occurrence;
(b) public liability insurance with a limit of not less than Naira One Hundred Million (₦100,000,000) per occurrence; and
(c) professional indemnity insurance with a limit of not less than Naira Fifty Million (₦50,000,000) per claim.
The Provider shall, upon request, provide the Client with certificates or other evidence of such insurance cover.

---

ARTICLE 10: CONFIDENTIALITY

10.1 For the purposes of this Agreement, "Confidential Information" means all information, whether oral, written, or in any other form, disclosed by or on behalf of one Party (the "Disclosing Party") to the other Party (the "Receiving Party"), whether before or after the Commencement Date, including but not limited to:
(a) the terms of this Agreement;
(b) any commercial, financial, technical, or operational information relating to the Disclosing Party's business, clients, suppliers, or personnel;
(c) any information relating to the Client's premises, recipes, menus, suppliers, pricing, or customer data; and
(d) any information which is marked as confidential or which would reasonably be understood to be confidential given its nature and the circumstances of disclosure.
